# Keep this whole host out of the search index.
#
# link/staginglink/devlink.geniegenerate.com are deep-link THRESHOLDS, not
# content. Every path rewrites to the same index.html (see _redirects), so a
# crawler finds one page duplicated across three hosts, all three competing in
# the SERP with the real marketing site at www.geniegenerate.com. Google had
# indexed link. and devlink. as standalone results for the query
# "geniegenerate" — devlink. still serving placeholder copy ("Premium hosting
# solutions... 99.9% uptime guarantee") from a revision that died long ago.
#
# `X-Robots-Tag`, not a robots.txt Disallow — same reasoning as the house rule
# in web/apps/site/app/robots.ts: a crawler BLOCKED from fetching the page can
# never read the noindex it was blocked from, and Google keeps a
# blocked-but-linked URL in the index as a bare title. It has to be let in to
# be told to leave. robots.txt here therefore says Allow.
#
# `/*` is required, not lazy: /shop/<uuid>, /v/<token>, /verify-email etc. all
# serve the same index.html and are all indexable without it.
#
# Safe on the two well-known files below: Apple's CDN and Google's Digital
# Asset Links verifier are not search crawlers and do not read robots
# directives. This does NOT weaken Universal Links or App Links verification.
/*
  X-Robots-Tag: noindex

# Apple and Google fetch the well-known files with strict expectations.
# Google's assetlinks verifier requires Content-Type: application/json.
# Apple's AASA verifier accepts application/json (and tolerates octet-stream
# in unsigned mode); application/json is the safe default.

/.well-known/apple-app-site-association
  Content-Type: application/json
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=300

/.well-known/assetlinks.json
  Content-Type: application/json
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=300
